You are working on the ExamFlow project.

We are now starting:

PHASE 7 — PERSONALIZED EXAM GENERATION

The goal of this phase is to take:

1. The active Crowdmark exam template
2. The exam roster
3. The confirmed seating assignments

and generate one personalized examination document for each student.

Each generated exam document must contain the student's:

- Name
- Student ID
- Exam
- Building
- Room
- Seat

while preserving the original Crowdmark exam content.

The generated document will later be used as the physical exam paper.

IMPORTANT:

This phase must be deterministic and document-oriented.

Do NOT use AI.

Do NOT use OCR.

Do NOT modify exam questions.

Do NOT implement grading.

Do NOT implement Crowdmark API integration.

Do NOT implement automatic submission to Crowdmark.

Do NOT implement seating assignment.

Do NOT implement seating maps.

Do NOT implement signature lists.

Do NOT implement bulk printing.

Do NOT implement QR codes yet unless the existing PRD explicitly defines
the QR payload and integration contract.

QR code integration should remain deferred to the next phase if the contract
is not already defined.

==================================================
1. READ PROJECT DOCUMENTATION
==================================================

Before coding, read:

- doc/prd.md
- doc/Architecture.md
- doc/DATABASE.md
- doc/SECURITY.md
- doc/DEVELOPMENT.md
- doc/AGENTS.md

Also inspect all previous phases:

- Phase 1 Authentication
- Phase 2 Exam Management
- Phase 3 Student/Roster
- Phase 4 Room/Seat
- Phase 5 Seating Assignment
- Phase 6 Crowdmark Template Import

Do not redesign existing modules.

Follow established conventions.

Before coding, provide a concise implementation plan.

==================================================
2. CORE WORKFLOW
==================================================

The workflow is:

Active Crowdmark Template
        +
Confirmed Seat Assignments
        +
Exam Roster
        ↓
Personalized Exam Generation
        ↓
One document per student

Example:

Template:

CSC108-final.pdf

Student:

Alice Student
100000001

Assignment:

IB101
A01

Generated document:

CSC108-final-100000001.pdf

The generated document contains:

Name: Alice Student
Student ID: 100000001
Room: IB 101
Seat: A01

followed by the original exam content.

==================================================
3. SOURCE OF TRUTH
==================================================

The generated document must use authoritative database data.

Student:

Exam roster

Room:

Exam seat assignment

Seat:

Exam seat assignment

Template:

Active ExamTemplate

Do NOT accept student/room/seat information from the frontend as authoritative.

==================================================
4. GENERATION PRECONDITIONS
==================================================

Before generation, verify:

1. Exam exists.
2. Exam has an active template.
3. Exam has a confirmed seating assignment.
4. Every roster student has exactly one assignment.
5. Every assignment has a valid room.
6. Every assignment has a valid usable seat.
7. Every assignment belongs to the exam.
8. Template file exists.
9. Template is readable.
10. Student roster is valid.

If any condition fails:

do not generate documents.

Return a clear validation error.

==================================================
5. NO PARTIAL GENERATION
==================================================

Generation should be treated as a batch operation.

Example:

185 students

If generation fails for student 117:

do not silently produce:

116 successful PDFs
69 missing PDFs

unless the architecture explicitly supports resumable jobs.

The initial implementation should prefer:

validate everything first
→ generate batch
→ finalize only if successful

If individual file generation must be streamed to storage, track generation
state explicitly.

Do not claim the batch succeeded unless all required outputs succeeded.

==================================================
6. GENERATION MODEL
==================================================

Create a GeneratedExam entity.

Suggested fields:

id
exam_id
student_id
assignment_id
template_id
file_name
storage_key
file_size
file_hash
status
generation_version
created_at
updated_at

Use existing project conventions.

==================================================
7. GENERATION STATUS
==================================================

Keep status simple.

Possible:

GENERATED
FAILED
ARCHIVED

If the existing architecture can use:

PENDING
GENERATING
GENERATED
FAILED

that is acceptable for asynchronous processing.

Do not create an unnecessarily complicated workflow.

==================================================
8. TEMPLATE VERSION

A generated document must record which template version was used.

Example:

Exam:
CSC108 Final

Template:
v3

Generated exam:

Student 100000001
Template v3

This is important for auditability.

==================================================
9. ASSIGNMENT VERSION

If Phase 5 provides assignment version information, record it.

This allows the system to explain:

"This document was generated from seating assignment version 2."

If Phase 5 did not implement assignment versions, do not introduce a complex
versioning system now.

Use the assignment record itself.

==================================================
10. FILE IDENTITY

The generated file must have a stable internal identity.

Do not use:

student name

as the database primary key.

Use the existing ID strategy.

==================================================
11. FILE NAMING

Use a safe deterministic filename.

Recommended concept:

{exam-code}-{student-id}-v{generation-version}.pdf

Example:

CSC108-FINAL-100000001-v1.pdf

Do not use student names in filenames unless explicitly required.

Student names may contain:

spaces
commas
apostrophes
Unicode
special characters

Avoid unnecessary filename complexity.

==================================================
12. STUDENT INFORMATION

The personalized exam should contain:

Student Name
Student ID
Exam
Room
Seat

Optional:

Course Code
Course Title
Exam Date

Only include fields explicitly required by doc/prd.md.

Do not duplicate information unnecessarily.

==================================================
13. HEADER DESIGN

Create a consistent exam identification area.

Example:

------------------------------------------------
CSC108 — Final Examination

Student Name: Alice Student
Student ID: 100000001

Room: IB 101
Seat: A01
------------------------------------------------

Then:

[Original Crowdmark exam content]

The exact visual design should be simple, professional, and printable.

==================================================
14. PRESERVE ORIGINAL CONTENT

The system must not modify:

- exam questions
- diagrams
- formulas
- images
- page ordering
- original content

unless required to place the personalized header.

The template should be treated as immutable source content.

==================================================
15. PDF GENERATION

Use a reliable PDF/document processing approach.

Prefer Python for PDF processing if the existing architecture already has a
Python backend/worker.

Do not introduce multiple PDF libraries unnecessarily.

Use the smallest reliable toolchain capable of:

- reading the source PDF
- adding a header/overlay
- producing a valid PDF
- preserving original pages

==================================================
16. DO NOT REBUILD THE PDF

Do not extract text from the original PDF and reconstruct it.

That risks:

- lost fonts
- lost diagrams
- changed pagination
- broken formulas
- changed layout

Instead:

overlay personalized information onto the original PDF.

==================================================
17. PDF OVERLAY

Preferred approach:

Source PDF
    ↓
Generate overlay PDF
    ↓
Merge overlay + source
    ↓
Personalized PDF

The original pages remain intact.

==================================================
18. OVERLAY LOCATION

The personalized information should be placed in a predictable location.

Default:

top of first page

unless the source template has an established reserved header area.

Do not obscure exam questions.

If the template is too close to the top margin:

detect/report the problem.

Do not silently cover existing exam content.

==================================================
19. TEMPLATE VALIDATION

Before generation:

inspect the first page dimensions.

Determine:

page width
page height

Use those dimensions to position the overlay.

Do not assume:

Letter size

unless the project requirements specify it.

Support the actual template page size.

==================================================
20. PAGE SIZE

The overlay must match the original page dimensions.

If source page is:

Letter

overlay is Letter.

If source page is:

A4

overlay is A4.

Do not resize the original document.

==================================================
21. PRINTING SAFETY

The personalized header must remain inside a reasonable printable area.

Do not place text directly at the physical edge of the page.

Use configurable margins.

Example:

PDF_MARGIN_TOP
PDF_MARGIN_LEFT
PDF_MARGIN_RIGHT

Do not hard-code coordinates in many places.

==================================================
22. FONT

Use a reliable embedded or standard PDF font.

The chosen font must support:

- student names
- normal institutional text
- common Unicode characters

Do not assume every student's name is ASCII.

If the current implementation cannot guarantee Unicode support, document the
limitation and use a suitable Unicode-capable font.

==================================================
23. FONT SIZE

Keep the header readable when printed.

Do not make student information extremely small.

Suggested hierarchy:

Exam title:
larger

Student name:
normal/medium

Student ID:
normal

Room/Seat:
normal

Keep the design conservative and professional.

==================================================
24. LONG STUDENT NAMES

The generator must handle long names.

For example:

Alexandru Constantin Popescu-Smith

Do not allow the name to overflow into other fields.

Use:

- text wrapping
- adaptive width
- truncation only as a last resort

Do not truncate student identity without a clear reason.

==================================================
25. UNICODE

Test names such as:

José García

François Dupont

Müller

李明

张伟

if supported by the system's student data.

The generated PDF must remain readable.

==================================================
26. STUDENT ID

Student ID must be treated as a string.

Do NOT convert it to an integer.

This prevents:

- leading zero loss
- formatting changes
- precision issues

==================================================
27. ROOM DISPLAY

Use a human-readable room label.

Example:

IB 101

not:

room_id = 92837

==================================================
28. SEAT DISPLAY

Use the physical seat label.

Example:

A01

Do not expose database seat IDs.

==================================================
29. GENERATION SERVICE

Create a dedicated service.

Conceptually:

PersonalizedExamGenerator

Responsibilities:

- validate inputs
- load template
- load student
- load assignment
- generate overlay
- merge PDF
- calculate hash
- store output
- return metadata

Do not put PDF logic inside API route handlers.

==================================================
30. STORAGE ABSTRACTION

Reuse the storage abstraction from Phase 6.

Do not create another unrelated storage system.

Example:

ExamTemplateStorage
GeneratedExamStorage

They may share a common storage interface.

==================================================
31. OUTPUT STORAGE

Generated exam documents must NOT be stored in:

public/
static/
frontend assets/

They contain student information.

Use protected storage.

==================================================
32. GENERATED FILE HASH

Calculate SHA-256 for generated documents.

Store:

file_hash

This provides integrity verification.

==================================================
33. GENERATION VERSION

If a document is regenerated:

do not silently overwrite the previous generated document.

Recommended:

generation version 1
generation version 2

The exact versioning strategy should remain simple.

==================================================
34. REGENERATION

Regeneration should require explicit confirmation.

Example:

"This will regenerate personalized exams using template version 3 and the
current seating assignments."

The user must confirm.

==================================================
35. IMPORTANT REGENERATION RULE

If seating assignments changed after previous generation:

the system should clearly indicate that existing personalized exams may be
outdated.

Do not silently continue using old assignment data.

==================================================
36. STALE DOCUMENT DETECTION

If practical, store:

template_id
assignment_id

or equivalent version references.

Then detect:

template changed
OR
assignment changed

and mark generated documents as stale.

Do not implement an elaborate synchronization engine.

==================================================
37. GENERATION BATCH

The user should be able to select:

[ Generate Personalized Exams ]

The system should display:

Students:
185

Template:
v3

Assignments:
v2

Expected documents:
185

Then:

[ Generate ]

==================================================
38. GENERATION PROGRESS

If generation is synchronous and small:

a normal request may be acceptable.

If generation is expected to take significant time:

use the existing worker/background-job architecture.

Do not introduce a new queue system just for this feature.

Reuse existing infrastructure if available.

==================================================
39. ASYNC JOB

If using a worker:

Create a generation job.

Example:

GENERATING

Then:

185 documents

Progress:

72 / 185

Status:

Generating personalized exams...

Do not block the web request for a long-running batch.

==================================================
40. JOB IDEMPOTENCY

A generation job should not accidentally produce duplicate documents.

Use:

exam_id
student_id
template_id
assignment_id
generation_version

to establish the generation context.

==================================================
41. FAILED GENERATION

If a student document fails:

record:

student
exam
error code
safe error message
timestamp

Do not expose internal stack traces to users.

==================================================
42. RETRY

If asynchronous generation is implemented:

allow safe retry of failed documents.

Do not regenerate successful documents unnecessarily.

Keep retry logic simple.

==================================================
43. GENERATION SUMMARY

Display:

Students:
185

Generated:
185

Failed:
0

Stale:
0

==================================================
44. GENERATED EXAM LIST

Create:

/app/exams/[id]/documents

Display:

Student ID
Student Name
Room
Seat
Template Version
Status
Generated Date

Actions:

Download

Do not expose raw storage paths.

==================================================
45. SEARCH

Allow search by:

- student ID
- student name
- room
- seat

Server-side search preferred.

==================================================
46. DOWNLOAD API

Implement:

GET
/api/generated-exams/{id}/download

Requirements:

- authentication
- authorization
- IDOR protection
- streaming
- safe filename
- correct content type

==================================================
47. BULK DOWNLOAD

Do NOT implement a ZIP download yet unless explicitly required by doc/prd.md.

If bulk download is required:

implement it later as a separate controlled feature.

Do not load hundreds of PDFs into memory.

==================================================
48. PDF SECURITY

Generated documents contain student information.

Do not:

- expose them publicly
- cache them in public CDN
- log their contents
- place them in static directories

Use secure response headers where appropriate.

==================================================
49. ACCESS CONTROL

Suggested:

ADMIN:
- generate
- regenerate
- download

STAFF:
- generate if authorized
- download if authorized

INSTRUCTOR:
- view/download documents for authorized exams

Use existing authorization model.

Do not create a second permission system.

==================================================
50. IDOR PROTECTION

Test:

User A has access to Exam A.

User A changes:

generated_exam_id

to a document belonging to Exam B.

Request must fail.

==================================================
51. AUDIT EVENTS

Add:

PERSONALIZED_EXAMS_GENERATION_STARTED
PERSONALIZED_EXAMS_GENERATION_COMPLETED
PERSONALIZED_EXAM_GENERATED
PERSONALIZED_EXAM_GENERATION_FAILED
PERSONALIZED_EXAM_DOWNLOADED

Audit data:

actor
exam_id
template_id
assignment context
timestamp
safe summary

Do not put the complete student roster into audit logs.

==================================================
52. NO STUDENT DATA IN LOGS

Application logs must not contain:

student names
student IDs
complete room/seat mappings
PDF contents

Use internal IDs and counts where possible.

==================================================
53. DATABASE

Create migration for:

generated_exams

Include appropriate:

- primary keys
- foreign keys
- indexes
- status
- hashes
- timestamps
- template reference
- assignment reference

==================================================
54. DATABASE CONSTRAINTS

Prevent accidental duplicates.

At minimum consider uniqueness around:

exam_id
student_id
template_id
assignment_id
generation_version

Use the simplest constraint compatible with the chosen regeneration model.

==================================================
55. DATA INTEGRITY

Before generating:

Verify:

assignment.student_id == roster student
assignment.exam_id == current exam
assignment.room belongs to exam
assignment.seat belongs to room
seat is usable
template belongs to exam

Any mismatch must stop generation.

==================================================
56. TEMPLATE INTEGRITY

Verify the template file hash when appropriate.

If the stored file is missing or corrupted:

generation must fail safely.

Do not generate from an unexpected file.

==================================================
57. PDF VALIDATION

After generation:

verify that the resulting file is a valid PDF.

If practical:

verify:

- file exists
- non-zero size
- valid PDF structure
- expected page count

Do not rely only on file extension.

==================================================
58. PAGE COUNT

Normally:

generated page count

should equal:

source template page count

unless the implementation intentionally adds pages.

Do not silently change page count.

==================================================
59. ORIGINAL TEMPLATE IMMUTABILITY

Never modify the source template.

Generation always creates a new output document.

The Crowdmark template must remain unchanged.

==================================================
60. TESTING — BASIC

Test:

1. One student.
2. One template.
3. One assignment.
4. Generate PDF.
5. Verify PDF exists.
6. Verify metadata.
7. Verify SHA-256.
8. Verify original template unchanged.

==================================================
61. TESTING — MULTIPLE STUDENTS

Test:

10 students.

Verify:

10 documents.

Each student receives:

exactly one document.

Each document contains the correct:

name
student ID
room
seat

==================================================
62. TESTING — DATA ISOLATION

Verify:

Student A's document does not contain Student B's information.

This is a critical security test.

==================================================
63. TESTING — TEMPLATE PRESERVATION

Compare:

source template page count
generated page count

Verify original content remains intact.

==================================================
64. TESTING — UNICODE

Test multilingual names.

Verify generated PDFs remain readable.

==================================================
65. TESTING — LONG NAMES

Test long student names.

Verify:

no overflow
no collision
no corrupted PDF

==================================================
66. TESTING — MISSING DATA

Test:

missing template
missing assignment
missing student
missing room
missing seat
missing file

All must fail safely.

==================================================
67. TESTING — INSUFFICIENT ASSIGNMENTS

If:

185 roster students

but:

184 assignments

generation must fail.

Do not generate 184 documents and silently ignore one student.

==================================================
68. TESTING — STALE ASSIGNMENT

Generate documents.

Then change seating assignment.

Attempt generation again.

The system should detect that the previous documents may be stale and require
explicit regeneration.

==================================================
69. TESTING — TEMPLATE CHANGE

Generate using:

template v1

Then activate:

template v2

The system must not silently pretend that existing documents were generated
from v2.

==================================================
70. TESTING — AUTHORIZATION

Test:

- unauthenticated generation
- unauthorized generation
- authorized generation
- unauthorized download
- authorized download

==================================================
71. TESTING — IDOR

Test:

generated exam ID from another exam.

Must fail.

==================================================
72. TESTING — STORAGE FAILURE

Simulate output storage failure.

Verify:

database state does not claim the document was successfully generated.

==================================================
73. TESTING — TRANSACTION

If generation metadata insertion fails:

do not leave inconsistent database records.

Clean up generated files where appropriate.

==================================================
